018,35702,839,62,17,379,15170,18,27275,59,2533,3136,3696,10607,1124,11520,839,62,23,379,284,1124,37018,35702,839,62,17,379,15170,18,11035,8,1119,279,23606,11,582,633,510,59,9640,59,839,62,17,1124,2359,7,1124,37018,90,88,15170,18,92,1124,1291,8,284,1124,37018,35702,839,62,17,379,15170,18,27275,59,21675,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,2610,1231,312,18883,55856,279,3491,323,18130,279,6291,504,279,7167,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind $(\\log_2 x)^2$ if $\\log_2 (\\log_8 x) = \\log_8 (\\log_2 x).$\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve the problem, we start with the given equation:\n\\[\n\\log_2 (\\log_8 x) = \\log_8 (\\log_2 x).\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's introduce a substitution to simplify the equation. Let:\n\\[\ny = \\log_2 x.\n\\]\n\nUsing this substitution, we can rewrite the equation as:\n\\[\n\\log_2 (\\log_8 x) = \\log_8 y.\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to express \\(\\log_8 x\\) in terms of base 2 logarithms. Recall that:\n\\[\n\\log_8 x = \\frac{\\log_2 x}{\\log_2 8}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\log_2 8 = 3\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\log_8 x = \\frac{\\log_2 x}{3} = \\frac{y}{3}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(\\log_8 x = \\frac{y}{3}\\) back into the equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{y}{3} \\right) = \\log_8 y.\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to express \\(\\log_8 y\\) in terms of base 2 logarithms. Using the change of base formula, we have:\n\\[\n\\log_8 y = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{\\log_2 8} = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(\\log_8 y = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}\\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{y}{3} \\right) = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}.\n\\]\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"f3f42968b6ac957b21bb79b065bccf523f59479ef4dffa47fe2c742358f414aa"},"seconds":42.69166612625122,"seed":726330843233412354,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we start with the given equation:\n\\[\n\\log_2 (\\log_8 x) = \\log_8 (\\log_2 x).\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's use the change of base formula to express \\(\\log_8 x\\) and \\(\\log_8 (\\log_2 x)\\) in terms of base 2 logarithms. Recall that:\n\\[\n\\log_8 x = \\frac{\\log_2 x}{\\log_2 8} = \\frac{\\log_2 x}{3}.\n\\]\nSo, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{\\log_2 x}{3} \\right) = \\log_8 (\\log_2 x).\n\\]\n\nNext, we express \\(\\log_8 (\\log_2 x)\\) in terms of base 2 logarithms:\n\\[\n\\log_8 (\\log_2 x) = \\frac{\\log_2 (\\log_2 x)}{\\log_2 8} = \\frac{\\log_2 (\\log_2 x)}{3}.\n\\]\nThus, the equation now is:\n\\[\n\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{\\log_2 x}{3} \\right) = \\frac{\\log_2 (\\log_2 x)}{3}.\n\\]\n\nLet \\(y = \\log_2 x\\). Then the equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{y}{3} \\right) = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}.\n\\]\nUsing the logarithm property \\(\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{y}{3} \\right) = \\log_2 y - \\log_2 3\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\log_2 y - \\log_2 3 = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}.\n\\]\nTo eliminate the fraction, multiply through by 3:\n\\[\n3 \\log_2 y - 3 \\log_2 3 = \\log_2 y.\n\\]\nRearrange the terms to isolate \\(\\log_2 y\\):\n\\[\n3 \\log_2 y - \\log_2 y = 3 \\log_2 3,\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\log_2 y = 3 \\log_2 3,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\log_2 y = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\nSince \\(y = \\log_2 x\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\log_2 (\\log_2 x) = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\nExponentiating both sides with base 2, we get:\n\\[\n\\log_2 x = 3^{3/2} = 3 \\sqrt{3}.\n\\]\nThus, we need to find \\((\\log_2 x)^2\\):\n\\[\n(\\log_2 x)^2 = (3 \\sqrt{3})^2 = 9 \\cdot 3 = 27.\n\\]\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{27}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{27}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To solve the problem, we start with the given equation:\n\\[\n\\log_2 (\\log_8 x) = \\log_8 (\\log_2 x).\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's use the change of base formula to express \\(\\log_8 x\\) and \\(\\log_8 (\\log_2 x)\\) in terms of base 2 logarithms. Recall that:\n\\[\n\\log_8 x = \\frac{\\log_2 x}{\\log_2 8} = \\frac{\\log_2 x}{3}.\n\\]\nSo, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{\\log_2 x}{3} \\right) = \\log_8 (\\log_2 x).\n\\]\n\nNext, we express \\(\\log_8 (\\log_2 x)\\) in terms of base 2 logarithms:\n\\[\n\\log_8 (\\log_2 x) = \\frac{\\log_2 (\\log_2 x)}{\\log_2 8} = \\frac{\\log_2 (\\log_2 x)}{3}.\n\\]\nThus, the equation now is:\n\\[\n\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{\\log_2 x}{3} \\right) = \\frac{\\log_2 (\\log_2 x)}{3}.\n\\]\n\nLet \\(y = \\log_2 x\\). Then the equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{y}{3} \\right) = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}.\n\\]\nUsing the logarithm property \\(\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{y}{3} \\right) = \\log_2 y - \\log_2 3\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\log_2 y - \\log_2 3 = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}.\n\\]\nTo eliminate the fraction, multiply through by 3:\n\\[\n3 \\log_2 y - 3 \\log_2 3 = \\log_2 y.\n\\]\nRearrange the terms to isolate \\(\\log_2 y\\):\n\\[\n3 \\log_2 y - \\log_2 y = 3 \\log_2 3,\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\log_2 y = 3 \\log_2 3,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\log_2 y = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\nSince \\(y = \\log_2 x\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\log_2 (\\log_2 x) = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\nExponentiating both sides with base 2, we get:\n\\[\n\\log_2 x = 3^{3/2} = 3 \\sqrt{3}.\n\\]\nThus, we need to find \\((\\log_2 x)^2\\):\n\\[\n(\\log_2 x)^2 = (3 \\sqrt{3})^2 = 9 \\cdot 3 = 27.\n\\]\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{27}.\n\\]","id":"intermediate_algebra:434"}
